    "long_instructions": "# Data Stories\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/009ab39825563cff628bf976699c3e10-image-1724342741218.png\" alt=\"scale icon\" width=\"250px\" style=\"border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 20px 0px 40px;\">\r\n\r\n#### Once we have good questions, the next step is communicating what we find. A data story combines visuals (like charts or graphs) with words to explain something in a way that others can understand. \r\n\r\n#### Some stories are **expository** —describing trends or telling what the data shows. Others are **persuasive**, aiming to convince the audience to take a stance or support an idea using evidence from data.\r\n\r\n#### In the classroom, students practice forming and refining data questions, and explore how stories built from data can inform or persuade. By analyzing real examples, they learn how to structure their own stories in ways that are clear, ethical, and engaging.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 📝 To Do\r\n\r\n#### In this lesson, your students analyze different expository and persuasive data stories. Two are pictured below.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: 20px; margin-top: 20px;\">\r\n\t<div style=\"background-color: #ffffff; box-shadow: 0px 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3); padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; width: 45%;\">\r\n    <p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><b>Expository Data Story Example</b></p>\r\n    <p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><i>Tells a story about a person, place, thing, or event using data</i></p>\r\n\t<br>\r\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;\">\r\n    \t<a href=\"https://studio.code.org/s/intro-to-data-science-2024/lessons/7/levels/1\" target=\"blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://images.code.org/47623db410174d08aeaea4da4648ef50-image-1733781085199.50.46 PM.png\" alt=\"Three cards with images and corresponding text show a user their listening style. The titles are Vampire, Alchemist, and Time Traveler.\" style=\"width: 100%; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></a>\r\n\t</div>\r\n        <p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 12px\">Click the image above to see a larger version.</p>\r\n\t\t<br>\r\n\t\t<p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><b>Spotify Year Wrapped</b> cards tell the user how their listening habits throughout the year are unique.</p>\r\n    </div>\r\n\t<div style=\"background-color: #ffffff; box-shadow: 0px 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3); padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; width: 45%;\">\r\n\t<p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><b>Persuasive Data Story Example</b></p>\r\n    <p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\"><i>Attempts to convince the audience of a stance based on evidence from data and visualizations</i></p>\r\n    <br>\r\n\t<div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;\">   \r\n\t\t<a href=\"https://studio.code.org/s/intro-to-data-science-2024/lessons/7/levels/2\" target=\"blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://images.code.org/84dae9bcc77482c22ab70dce932715e4-image-1733781085193.51.04 PM.png\" alt=\"An infographic demonstrates Lebron James' basketball achievements.\" style=\"width: 100%;  margin-bottom: 10px;\"></a>\r\n    </div>\r\n    \t<p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 12px\">Click the image above to see a larger version.</p>\r\n\t\t<br>\r\n        <p style=\"color: black; line-height: 1.25; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; font-size: 18px\">This <b>Lebron James</b> infographic aims to persuade the reader to recognize the player's many accomplishments.</p>\r\n\t</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n#### Let's look at the **expository example**. \r\n\r\n\r\n#### Choose one character card from the Spotify Year Wrapped Expository Data Story example and reflect on the following questions:\r\n- #### *What story does this card tell us about the user?*\r\n- #### *What data attributes might be associated with this story?*\r\n\r\n#### Remember, data attributes are specific characteristics or pieces of information, like age, location, or preferences, that help describe the user or their actions.\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/21f770ddc3577c930c185ce29618d465-image-1731094397180.png\" alt=\"design process graph of the cycle\" width=\"750px\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\r\n<br>",
